              },
              {
                "from": "f",
                "to": "c"
              },
              {
                "from": "h",
                "to": "m",
                "label": "ref"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A ==branch== is a 40-byte file holding one SHA.",
            "`HEAD` names a branch; ==detached HEAD== names a commit.",
            "Commits are **nodes**; parents are the edges.",
            "Merge commit = two parents, so it is a graph."
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The three tools, three questions",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Question",
            "Tool",
            "Touches"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "I want this change here",
              "cherry-pick",
              "patch"
            ],
            [
              "Which change broke it",
              "bisect",
              "history"
            ],
            [
              "Where did my commit go",
              "reflog",
              "pointers"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git cherry-pick 4ad81e   # replay diff",
            "git bisect start         # binsearch",
            "git reflog               # HEAD log"
          ],
          "note": "one page each: pages 2, 4, 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "{green:cherry-pick} = replay one commit's diff (or `A^..B`) onto HEAD.",
            "{purple:bisect} = binary search the DAG for the __first__ flip good`->`bad.",
            "{orange:reflog} = local per-ref journal of every value HEAD/branch held."
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setting",
            "Default",
            "Applies to"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "gc.reflogExpire",
              "90 days",
              "reachable"
            ],
            [
              "...ExpireUnreachable",
              "30 days",
              "orphans"
            ],
            [
              "git gc --prune=now",
              "instant",
              "all loose"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1,
          "frame": "dashed"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "git",
      "subtitle": "Objects, refs, history"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cherry-pick: Replaying a Commit",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Reachable vs existing",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "chain",
          "spec": {
            "title": "Life of an orphaned commit",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "Object written to .git/objects",
                "note": "exists, has a SHA",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "reset --hard: no branch reaches it",
                "note": "unreachable, still there",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "reflog entry keeps it alive",
                "note": "HEAD@{1} finds it",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "gc after expiry deletes it",
                "note": "now really gone",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Mental model for the book",
          "text": "Three layers: ==patches== (content diffs, cherry-pick) vs ==pointers== (refs, reflog) vs ==history== (the DAG, bisect). **Every** command in this handbook touches exactly one."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky-pink",
          "title": "Terminology trap",
          "text": "\"Lost commits\" are __not lost__ until `git gc` runs. They are only **unreachable** — and a fresh clone has no reflog to rescue them."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What It Does",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`git cherry-pick <sha>` diffs <sha> against its ==parent== and applies that patch onto HEAD as a __three-way merge__, then commits with the original message and author.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Field",
            "After pick"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "SHA",
              "**new**"
            ],
            [
              "parent",
              "new (your HEAD)"
            ],
            [
              "committer date",
              "now"
            ],
            [
              "author + date",
              "preserved"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Mechanism: Ranges",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git cherry-pick B        # one",
            "git cherry-pick A..B     # A excluded!",
            "git cherry-pick A^..B    # A included",
            "git cherry-pick -m 1 <merge>"
          ],
          "note": "off-by-one on A..B is the classic bug"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`A..B` = everything **after** A, up to B",
            "`A^..B` starts at A's parent, so A is in",
            "Merge commit has no single diff: `-m 1` names the ==mainline== parent",
            "`-m 2` diffs against the side branch instead"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Flags Worth Knowing",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`-n`",
              "stage only, no commit"
            ],
            [
              "`-x`",
              "add \"(cherry picked from...)\""
            ],
            [
              "`-e`",
              "edit message"
            ],
            [
              "`-s`",
              "add Signed-off-by"
            ],
            [
              "`--ff`",
              "fast-forward if HEAD is parent"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "-n squashes",
          "text": "Pick several with `-n`, then one `git commit` folds them into ==one== commit."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# what is not upstream yet?",
            "git cherry -v upstream branch",
            "# '+' = missing, '-' = already there"
          ],
          "note": "read-only scout, patch-id matched"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "git",
      "subtitle": "Copy diff onto HEAD"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cherry-pick vs Merge: Copy or Link",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Example: Backport a Fix",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git checkout release-1.4",
            "git cherry-pick -x 9fceb02",
            "git push origin release-1.4"
          ],
          "note": "-x is mandatory on release branches"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Conflicts & Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "conflict",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "fix files"
              },
              {
                "text": "git add"
              },
              {
                "text": "--continue",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--continue` reuses the message in ==.git/sequencer== — do **not** re-name the commit",
            "`--abort` restores the full pre-pick state",
            "`--skip` drops only that one commit, keeps going",
            "Forgot `A^`? Commit A silently missing from the range",
            "No `-x` on a backport? Provenance gone forever"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Copy, not move",
          "text": "The source commit is untouched; the patch now exists **twice** under two SHAs. Page 3 covers what that costs you."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What Each Tool Does",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Merge==: joins two histories, adds a merge commit",
            "Git __records the relationship__ so a change applies **once**",
            "==Cherry-pick==: copies content only, records `no` relationship",
            "Same change now exists **twice**, under two SHAs"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git merge feat   # link recorded",
            "git cherry-pick c3  # copy only",
            "# c3 -> new SHA d9, no parent link"
          ],
          "note": "merge remembers, pick forgets"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Rebase Is Pick in a Loop",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git rebase main",
            "# = cherry-pick A..B onto main",
            "# then move branch ptr to tip"
          ],
          "note": "same sequencer machinery as pick"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Comparison: Which One",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Tool"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "hotfix backport to 1.x",
              "cherry-pick"
            ],
            [
              "salvage 1 commit, dead branch",
              "cherry-pick"
            ],
            [
              "fix needed before refactor",
              "cherry-pick"
            ],
            [
              "pull in a whole feature",
              "**merge**"
            ],
            [
              "reorder unpushed work",
              "`rebase -i`"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`rebase` and `git cherry` match by ==patch-id==, not SHA",
            "One edited line, one whitespace fix = ==no match==",
            "Then the patch lands **twice** and conflicts"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "git",
      "subtitle": "Which tool, when"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Revert, Duplicates and Bisect Setup",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Axis",
            "merge",
            "pick",
            "revert"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "history fidelity",
              "high",
              "**low**",
              "n/a"
            ],
            [
              "conflict cost",
              "once",
              "every branch",
              "once"
            ],
            [
              "auditability",
              "good",
              "poor w/o `-x`",
              "**best**"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Example: Duplicate Commit",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git cherry-pick abc  # -> def",
            "git merge feat   # abc arrives",
            "# same patch, 2 SHAs, conflict"
          ],
          "note": "patch-id drops it only if byte-identical"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Example: Reverting a Merge",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git revert -m 1 <merge-sha>",
            "git merge feat  # brings nothing",
            "git revert <revert-sha>  # undo"
          ],
          "note": "merge base still claims it merged"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Re-merging after a merge-revert is a **silent no-op**",
            "Cherry-picking a whole feature instead of merging it",
            "Rebasing shared branches: use ==revert== on public history"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Rule of thumb",
          "text": "==Cherry-pick== across long-lived branches that will **never** merge. ==Merge== across branches that **will**. ==Revert== on anything already pushed — it adds the inverse diff and deletes nothing."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What Bisect Solves",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bug appeared somewhere in ==N commits==, you don't know where",
            "Checking one by one is **O(N)**; bisect is `O(log2 N)`",
            "Git halves the suspect range on every answer you give",
            "Needs one __test__ you can run at any commit"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Range",
            "Linear",
            "Bisect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "30 commits",
              "30 runs",
              "~5 runs"
            ],
            [
              "300 commits",
              "300 runs",
              "~9 runs"
            ],
            [
              "10,000 commits",
              "10,000 runs",
              "~14 runs"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Monotonic or bust",
          "text": "Core assumption: once **bad**, always bad. A ==flaky test== or intermittent bug breaks correctness — bisect will name a confidently wrong commit."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Starting a Session",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git bisect start",
            "git bisect bad          # HEAD",
            "git bisect good v2.3.0  # old sha",
            "# Bisecting: 9 steps left"
          ],
          "note": "lands you in detached HEAD"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Verdict",
            "Meaning",
            "Git does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`good`",
              "bug absent",
              "search newer half"
            ],
            [
              "`bad`",
              "bug present",
              "search older half"
            ],
            [
              "`skip`",
              "untestable",
              "picks a neighbour"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "git",
      "subtitle": "Which tool, when"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Bisect: Binary Search Over History",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`bad` = current, broken; `good <sha>` = known working",
            "Git checks out a __midpoint__ in {orange:detached HEAD}",
            "Prints `roughly N steps` so you know the cost up front",
            "Shortcut: `git bisect start HEAD v2.3.0` sets both at once"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Loop",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "One bisect step",
            "rows": [
              [
                "a"
              ],
              [
                "b"
              ],
              [
                "c",
                "d",
                "e"
              ],
              [
                "f"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "a": {
                "text": "Git checks out midpoint",
                "shape": "box",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              "b": {
                "text": "Run your test",
                "shape": "diamond",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              "c": {
                "text": "bisect good",
                "shape": "pill",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              "d": {
                "text": "bisect bad",
                "shape": "pill",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              "e": {
                "text": "bisect skip",
                "shape": "pill",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              "f": {
                "text": "X is the first bad commit",
                "shape": "box",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            },
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "a",
                "to": "b"
              },
              {
                "from": "b",
                "to": "c",
                "label": "works"
              },
              {
                "from": "b",
                "to": "d",
                "label": "broken"
              },
              {
                "from": "b",
                "to": "e",
                "label": "won't build"
              },
              {
                "from": "c",
                "to": "f"
              },
              {
                "from": "d",
                "to": "f"
              },
              {
                "from": "e",
                "to": "f"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. On a DAG, Not a Line",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "With merges there is no single 'middle' commit",
            "Git picks the commit that best __halves the ancestor set__ still suspect",
            "Non-linear history is handled ==natively== — no flattening needed",
            "`git bisect visualize` (or `view`) opens gitk on the suspects"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Session Control",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git bisect log > b.log  # dump",
            "git bisect replay b.log # redo",
            "git bisect reset        # exit"
          ],
          "note": "reset restores your original branch"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Mis-typed `good` for `bad`? edit `b.log`, then **replay**",
            "`reset` returns HEAD to the branch and ref you started on",
            "`--term-old=fast --term-new=slow` renames the verdicts",
            "Use renamed terms for perf regressions or 'when did this appear'"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git bisect start \\",
            "  --term-old=fast --term-new=slow",
            "git bisect slow; git bisect fast"
          ],
          "note": "same search, non-bug vocabulary"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Next page",
          "text": "`git bisect run <cmd>` does the whole loop for you — exit codes decide {green:good} / {red:bad} / {orange:skip}."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Mechanism: exit codes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`git bisect run <cmd>` runs your command at every step and answers ==good/bad== for you. Git reads only the __exit code__.",
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Exit",
            "Meaning",
            "Git does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "good",
              "mark good"
            ],
            [
              "1-124, 126, 127",
              "bad",
              "mark bad"
            ],
            [
              "125",
              "**cannot test**",
              "`skip`"
            ],
            [
              "128+",
              "abort",
              "session stops"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2,
          "frame": "solid"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "git",
      "subtitle": "O(log n) bug hunting"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Automated Bisect and Bisect in Anger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git bisect start HEAD v1.9",
            "git bisect run npm test -- \\",
            "  tests/checkout.spec.js",
            "# walks whole range unattended"
          ],
          "note": "start = bad HEAD, then good ref"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Probes you actually write",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# build failure is NOT a bug",
            "git bisect run sh -c '",
            "  make -j8 || exit 125",
            "  ./app --selftest'"
          ],
          "note": "125 keeps broken builds out"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# when did this string appear?",
            "git bisect run sh -c \\",
            "  '! grep -q deprecatedCall \\",
            "     src/api.ts'"
          ],
          "note": "grep hit = exit 1 = bad"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "#!/bin/sh   # /tmp/bisect-check.sh",
